      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-independent-random-variables-C016",
      "verdict": "partial",
      "gap": "The boundary asserts at line 400 that the extension's measurable-space hypotheses 'remain essential', but nothing on the route supports or attributes this. Durrett p. 53 (PDF p. 61, inside this unit's p. 53 locator) states that Theorem 2.1.21 is not valid for arbitrary measurable spaces, attributes the first counterexample (an infinite product of different spaces) to Andersen and Jessen (1948) with references to Halmos (1950) p. 214 and Neveu (1965) p. 84, and cites Wegner (1973) for an example with identical factor spaces. None of this is reported on the route: countable-product-measures.tex lines 263-278 and 295-307 only disclaim any extension for arbitrary measurable spaces. The same page's definition of a nice space (a one-to-one map into R with phi and phi^{-1} both measurable, with no Borel-image requirement) and its claims that this class is adequate for all of the book's results, that the generalization of Kolmogorov's theorem to it is trivial, and that most spaces arising in applications are nice are also not reported; the route substitutes Borel real codings with a Borel image (countable-product-measures.tex lines 159-182) and proves the extension only for them (lines 263-278). The only treatment of the failure claim and its attributions, def:p15-w02c-p15-004-extension-failure in handbook/parts/p15/measure-integration-and-product-constructions-w02c-p15-004.tex lines 339-355, is not input on the route from handbook/main.tex. Supply on the route the source's failure statement with its four attributions and Durrett's own nice-space definition and claims, with the p. 53 locator, and support or cite them from this boundary.",
